│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 1) Cash and cash equivalents                                                                         │
│ Examples: savings accounts, money market funds, Treasury bills, short-term CDs                          │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: capital preservation, liquidity, emergency funds                                             │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Lowest volatility                                                                                   │
│   - Easiest access to funds                                                                             │
│   - Usually lowest long-term return potential                                                           │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Inflation risk: purchasing power can erode over time                                                │
│   - Reinvestment risk: rates may fall when you renew                                                    │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Best thought of as stability and optionality, not growth.                                               │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 2) Bonds / fixed income                                                                              │
│ Examples: government bonds, municipal bonds, corporate bonds, bond funds                                │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: income, diversification, capital preservation relative to stocks                             │
│ - How they work:                                                                                        │
│   - You lend money to an issuer in exchange for interest payments                                       │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Usually less volatile than stocks, but not risk-free                                                │
│   - Returns often come from yield plus price changes                                                    │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Interest-rate risk: bond prices often fall when rates rise                                          │
│   - Credit risk: issuer may weaken or default                                                           │
│   - Inflation risk: fixed payments may lose real value                                                  │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Shorter-duration bonds tend to be less sensitive to rate changes than longer-duration bonds.            │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 3) Equities / stocks                                                                                 │
│ Examples: individual company shares, stock mutual funds, ETFs                                           │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: long-term growth, participation in corporate earnings                                        │
│ - How they work:                                                                                        │
│   - Ownership stake in a company                                                                        │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Higher long-term return potential                                                                   │
│   - Higher volatility and drawdown risk                                                                 │
│   - Some pay dividends; many rely mostly on price appreciation                                          │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Business risk                                                                                       │
│   - Valuation risk                                                                                      │
│   - Market risk                                                                                         │
│   - Sector concentration risk                                                                           │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Stocks are often split into styles and segments:                                                        │
│ - Large-cap vs mid-cap vs small-cap                                                                     │
│ - Growth vs value                                                                                       │
│ - Domestic vs international vs emerging markets                                                         │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 4) Real estate                                                                                       │
│ Examples: direct property ownership, REITs, real estate funds                                           │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: income, diversification, inflation sensitivity in some cases                                 │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Direct real estate can generate rent and potential appreciation                                     │
│   - REITs offer easier access and liquidity than owning property directly                               │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Property-specific risk                                                                              │
│   - Interest-rate sensitivity                                                                           │
│   - Illiquidity for direct ownership                                                                    │
│   - Leverage risk if financed with debt                                                                 │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Real estate often behaves differently from stocks and bonds, though listed REITs can still be quite     │
│ market-sensitive.                                                                                       │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 5) Commodities                                                                                       │
│ Examples: gold, oil, natural gas, agricultural products, broad commodity funds                          │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: diversification, inflation hedge in some environments                                        │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Can perform well during supply shocks or inflationary periods                                       │
│   - Often highly cyclical and volatile                                                                  │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - No cash flow for many commodities                                                                   │
│   - Prices can be driven by geopolitics, weather, and supply-demand imbalances                          │
│   - Futures-based products can have roll costs                                                          │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Gold is often treated separately because it is used by some investors as a store-of-value asset rather  │
│ than a productive asset.                                                                                │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 6) Cryptocurrencies / digital assets                                                                 │
│ Examples: Bitcoin, Ether, other tokens                                                                  │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: speculative growth, alternative store-of-value thesis, blockchain exposure                   │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Very high volatility                                                                                │
│   - 24/7 trading                                                                                        │
│   - Market structure and regulation still evolving                                                      │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Extreme price swings                                                                                │
│   - Regulatory uncertainty                                                                              │
│   - Custody/security risk                                                                               │
│   - Technology and adoption risk                                                                        │
│                                                                                                         │
│ Crypto is generally considered a high-risk asset class.                                                 │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 7) Alternatives                                                                                      │
│ Examples: private equity, venture capital, hedge funds, private credit, collectibles, infrastructure    │
│                                                                                                         │
│ - Purpose: diversification, specialized return streams, access to non-public markets                    │
│ - Typical traits:                                                                                       │
│   - Can behave differently from public markets                                                          │
│   - Often less liquid and more complex                                                                  │
│   - May have higher fees and less transparency                                                          │
│ - Main risks:                                                                                           │
│   - Illiquidity                                                                                         │
│   - Valuation opacity                                                                                   │
│   - Manager selection risk                                                                              │
│   - Long lock-up periods                                                                                │
│                                                                                                         │
│ “Alternatives” is a broad bucket, so risk can vary widely.                                              │
│                                                                                                         │
│ ## 8) Funds as wrappers, not asset classes                                                              │
│ Examples: mutual funds, ETFs, index funds                                                               │
│                                                                                                         │
│ These are not asset classes themselves. They are vehicles that hold asset classes.                      │
│                                                                                                         │
